From: rcllf local patch
Subject: [PATCH] reactor_common: defer a colliding event past every queued one

_lf_schedule_at_tag() handles an event that lands on a tag where the same
trigger already has a queued event. Under the default `defer` policy it
moved the new event one microstep later, without checking that tag. A
federate schedules every network message through this function. An `after d`
connection maps the tags (t,0), (t,1), (t,2) of one sender time to the
one tag (t+d,0), so the second message moved to (t+d,1) and the third also
moved to (t+d,1). Two events of one trigger at one tag set the port twice
before its reactions ran, and the third message was lost. lf_schedule_trigger()
(lib/schedule.c) already loops until a free microstep; this does the same.

Upstream reactor-c main still has the single bump (checked 2026-10-12).
---
--- a/core/reactor_common.c
+++ b/core/reactor_common.c
@@ -589,10 +589,13 @@
       return 0;
       break;
     default:
-      // Adding a microstep to the original
-      // intended tag.
-      tag.microstep++;
-      e->base.tag = tag;
+      // Adding a microstep to the original intended tag, past every
+      // event of this trigger already queued: two events of one trigger
+      // at one tag would leave only the last payload.
+      do {
+        tag.microstep++;
+        e->base.tag = tag;
+      } while (pqueue_tag_find_equal_same_tag(env->event_q, (pqueue_tag_element_t*)e) != NULL);
       if (lf_is_tag_after_stop_tag(env, tag)) {
         // Scheduling e will incur a microstep after the stop tag,
         // which is illegal.
